thing right now.

00:10:26.659 --> 00:10:28.700
MARC ANDREESSEN: For
example, this week, perhaps.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.199
QUENTIN HARDY: Depends on how
you feel about your democracy.

00:10:31.199 --> 00:10:33.930
Now, you were
saying to me earlier

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:37.140
that you are seeing
a lot more, I

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:40.080
hate saying conventional,
but historic businesses,

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.740
non-Valley businesses moving in
and taking a really active role

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:47.360
in learning about
the cloud directly.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.441
CEO straight to the
problem, learning about ML,

00:10:50.441 --> 00:10:51.940
how do they approach
it differently.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:54.310
What do they want from this?

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.880
MARC ANDREESSEN: I think
there's a history of it.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.667
A lot of big companies
got excited in the 1990s

00:10:59.667 --> 00:11:01.500
because the internet
had arrived and IBM had

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.791
e-business and the whole thing.

00:11:02.791 --> 00:11:06.005
Then, a lot of big companies
in 2000, 2001, 2002

00:11:06.005 --> 00:11:07.380
breathed a massive
sigh of relief

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.691
and said oh thank god that
internet thing didn't work.

00:11:10.691 --> 00:11:11.940
Stick a fork in it, it's done.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.523
Everybody knows the dotcom thing
was a bubble that was a joke,

00:11:14.523 --> 00:11:17.310
it's over and now we don't
have to worry about it.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.480
They maintained that view until,
I would say, about 2010, 2011.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.210
Then, I think it flipped pretty
much directly into panic.

00:11:27.114 --> 00:11:28.530
It's interesting
it also coincides

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:29.705
with the generational
change of leadership

00:11:29.705 --> 00:11:31.542
at a lot of the big
companies where they now

00:11:31.542 --> 00:11:33.750
