of leaders actually running

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.599
the companies, who
grew up with computers,

00:11:36.599 --> 00:11:37.890
unlike the previous generation.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:41.847
In the last six years, we've
seen a significant move

00:11:41.847 --> 00:11:43.680
and, more recently, a
flood of big companies

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.216
from incumbent industries
coming to Silicon Valley

00:11:46.216 --> 00:11:48.090
and trying to learn what
all this stuff means

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:49.260
and trying to figure
out how to use

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:50.700
it to either improve
their business

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:51.735
or defend against new attackers.

00:11:51.735 --> 00:11:53.280
QUENTIN HARDY: Are they
looking at--, sorry go ahead.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:54.090
MARC ANDREESSEN:
Correspondingly, we've

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.779
also seen the rise of entire new
generations of startups, that

00:11:56.779 --> 00:11:58.320
are now using these
new technologies,

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.870
to directly go after incumbent
industries that historically

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:02.720
have never been
threatened by technology

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.435
and never been threatened
by technology startups.

00:12:04.435 --> 00:12:05.940
QUENTIN HARDY: An
Airbnb essentially

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:09.140
models a hotel chain or an
Uber models a taxi fleet.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.610
MARC ANDREESSEN: The
basic calculus here

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.249
is that the tech
industry historically

00:12:13.249 --> 00:12:15.540
has been very good at having
a big impact on industries

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.280
in which there is
rapid technology

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.560
change, rapid productivity
change, that also turn out

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.060
to be industries
that 1, just aren't

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.700
that big relative to the
size of the economy and 2,

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:25.020
aren't that regulated.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.150
Media and entertainment,
consumer electronics,

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.990
retail, there's just not
that big as a percentage

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:31.834
of the economy right.

00:12:31.834 --> 00:12:33.750
Most of the economy is
health care, education,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:35.760
and construction, government.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.255
These giant industries that
are just big and gigantic

00:12:39.255 --> 00:12:42.776
and regulated and with
huge income and strength.

00:12:42.776 --> 00:12:44.900
It appears, or at least
what a lot of entrepreneurs

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.358
in the Valley believe, is that
the new technologies are now

00:12:47.358 --> 00:12:50.910
so powerful and so potent, if
you bring mobile, plus network

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:54.240
access, plus AI, plus
whatever, and you apply it

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.366
into an industry like real
estate or transportation

00:12:56.366 --> 00:12:58.031
or education or health
care, you can now

00:12:58.031 --> 00:12:59.350
actually have a big impact.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:01.511
It wasn't possible
in the past and so

00:13:01.511 --> 00:13:03.760
we're seeing the attackers
also shop at the same time.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.410
VINT CERF: I'm
actually going to argue

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.830
that big is not necessarily
the only reason the cloud is

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:09.740
of interest.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:12.350
The other reason is
continuous computation,

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:15.430
continuous gathering of
data, continuous observation

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:18.100
and analysis can make
a huge difference

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:19.390
in the medical world.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:22.390
For example, you only go to see
the doctor when you're sick,

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:25.570
so the doctors model of
you is you're always sick

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.460
and he never sees you
when you're healthy.

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:29.770
If you're monitoring
your body for example,

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:32.410
then you can get a baseline
that says this is what's normal.

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.100
Now they have the
ability to say,

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:35.419
uh oh something is not normal.

00:13:35.419 --> 00:13:37.960
But it only works because you
have this continuous monitoring

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:38.890
going on.

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:41.634
That's probably also true
for various kinds of business

00:13:41.634 --> 00:13:43.300
where you're trying
to understand what's

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:46.750
going on dynamically and being
able to see things in real time

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:49.070
makes a huge difference.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:51.700
We don't have time for it
in some of our advertising

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:52.690
mechanisms.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.370
We know in real time
how much people are

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:55.630
spending on a particular ad.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.940
We can stop showing
an ad when it runs out

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:00.520
of the limit that
the users have set

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.560
but that only works
if you have real time

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.699
computation of billing.

00:14:04.699 --> 00:14:06.490
QUENTIN HARDY: I think
just to circle back,

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:10.990
it's interesting to look at this
process of these new companies

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:13.330
modeling an industry
and then attacking it

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.400
because when they
do this, they don't

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.561
look at it to talk about how
different types of computing

00:14:18.561 --> 00:14:19.060
are.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.340
They don't look at the
toolset in a siloed way

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:24.670
if you look at a [INAUDIBLE]
or an Airbnb or a Snap,

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:27.214
there's mobility, there's
AI, there's all these things

00:14:27.214 --> 00:14:29.380
and they're not thinking
about them in a siloed way,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.360
they're thinking about
a very continuous way

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:33.940
with the objective
of using the best

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:37.530
capability towards their end.

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.460
MARC ANDREESSEN: And
they're not trying

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.777
to defend legacy,
investment, status quo.

00:14:41.777 --> 00:14:43.360
Business models are
now trying to find

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:46.420
big employee bases
that maybe do need

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:47.770
to fundamentally transform.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.452
It's asymmetric.

00:14:53.452 --> 00:14:54.910
The odds of success
of the attacker

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:58.600
are lower than the odds of the
defender defending successfully

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:00.687
but the challenge is
easier, attacking is easier.

00:15:00.687 --> 00:15:02.770
QUENTIN HARDY: And even
from a risk point of view,

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.301
if you learn something, you've
got an asset at the end of it

00:15:05.301 --> 00:15:05.800
anyway.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:06.190
MARC ANDREESSEN: Yeah, right.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:07.856
VINT CERF: You mentioned
business models

00:15:07.856 --> 00:15:10.000
and this raises an
interesting problem.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.980
Some companies build
their businesses

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:14.500
around the model, which
has been very successful.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:17.230
Newspapers here are an
example, and then the world

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:19.247
moves out from under them.

00:15:19.247 --> 00:15:20.830
One interesting
question is whether we

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:24.010
can use these computational
models to sort of help you A)

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:27.400
figure out whether not your
business model is failing,

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.730
or B) what alternative
business models could there be.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:34.870
The idea of being able to do
what-if simulations of a fairly

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:37.240
large scale model
of what's going on,

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.400
could be a very
powerful tool and only

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:42.300
a cloud computing environment
has the capacity to do that.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.050
MARC ANDREESSEN: I'll
give you an example.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.030
My friend Sam Larson has
this theory, basically

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:48.780
says historically in the economy
it felt like bits have always

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:51.280
moved faster than atoms but in
reality for most, the economy

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:52.680
atoms move faster than bits.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.907
Why do chain fast food
restaurants exist?

00:15:54.907 --> 00:15:57.490
It's because if you're a hungry
traveler at 9 o'clock at night

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.300
and see McDonald's sign,
you know what's there.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:00.925
And if you just see
random Joe's diner,

00:16:00.925 --> 00:16:03.040
you don't know if it's any
good or not, therefore,

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:05.265
this massive rise of
chains across the economy

00:16:05.265 --> 00:16:07.390
and this massive decline
of independent restaurants

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:09.937
and bars and everything else.

00:16:09.937 --> 00:16:12.520
What he says basically is, that
was just because it's actually

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:15.103
easier to get the atoms in place
in the form of the McDonald's

00:16:15.103 --> 00:16:17.800
sign than it was to transmit
the information about which

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:19.270
local restaurants are good.

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:20.860
In the new world, it flips.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:23.332
It's now easier to go on
Yelp or TripAdvisor, or any

00:16:23.332 --> 00:16:25.540
of these sites, or Airbnb
and be able to discover it.

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:27.100
No, I actually want to
stay in this apartment,

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:28.683
I want to go to this
local restaurant,

00:16:28.683 --> 00:16:30.980
I want to go to this local
bar, this local coffee shop

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:33.030
but I'm going to have
actually a better experience.

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:34.420
QUENTIN HARDY: It's
changing the consumer, too,

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:36.336
because they can sharpen
their desires better.

00:16:36.336 --> 00:16:37.900
It's not just the
generic McDonald's,

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:39.859
it's an interesting
experience they can derive.

00:16:39.859 --> 00:16:41.108
MARC ANDREESSEN: That's right.

00:16:41.108 --> 00:16:42.400
And then you get the feedback.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:44.660
you collect the data and
so you get the data back.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:47.190
You get the two-way ratings
on Uber and Airbnb and eBay

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:49.240
and you get the two-way
ratings, so, you actually have,

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:50.650
if anything, better
quality control

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:51.700
than the centralized management.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:53.783
VINT CERF: This raises a
very interesting question

00:16:53.783 --> 00:16:57.495
about the accuracy and
quality of the data

00:16:57.495 --> 00:16:59.980
and in some cases, the
