00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:01.990
Somebody who wants to
mess up your business

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.137
could conceivably go in
and monkey with the data,

00:17:04.137 --> 00:17:05.720
giving you the wrong
impression of how

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.300
your business is running.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.400
So, I think that
there are gotchas

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:12.460
that hide in this infrastructure
that we're going to have

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:16.193
to work hard to protect.

00:17:16.193 --> 00:17:17.859
QUENTIN HARDY: Who
knew in the new world

00:17:17.859 --> 00:17:21.235
it's still going to be
hard to be good, right?

00:17:21.235 --> 00:17:22.780
The humans.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:27.481
To speak to the data problem
and the large incumbent problem,

00:17:27.481 --> 00:17:29.230
the big incumbents
come and obviously they

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.090
have enormous amount of
data they want to use,

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.290
and they want to
generate new data,

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:34.060
and understand their
customers better,

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:37.270
what is the decisive
thing for a company?

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:40.300
Is it going to be the amount
of data, the quality of data,

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:43.435
the reliability of data,
or is it case by case?

00:17:43.435 --> 00:17:46.840
VINT CERF: My first reaction
is that all three have a role

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:48.340
to play here and
I would not want

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:51.190
to elevate one after the
other although, the quality

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.980
of the data and
it's reliability is

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:55.750
terribly important
because otherwise it's

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.690
garbage in, garbage out.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.260
But, I mentioned
earlier this notion

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.320
of being able to capture
things on a continuous basis.

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:05.380
That turns out to be
almost as important as all

00:18:05.380 --> 00:18:07.300
the other parameters
that you mentioned

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.702
because that continuity gives
you a sense for what's going on

00:18:10.702 --> 00:18:12.160
in a way that you
wouldn't normally

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:17.050
get if you have a rather slow
collection of information.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:18.760
MARC ANDREESSEN: I
would take speed.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:20.800
I think it's simply
speed of adaptation

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:23.440
because just look at the ways
you deal with data today.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:25.231
They're just completely
different than they

00:18:25.231 --> 00:18:26.327
were five years ago.

00:18:26.327 --> 00:18:28.660
The ability, for example, to
apply deep learning to data

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:31.210
is a brand new thing
and you indicated,

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.710
the young companies
that are growing

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:35.161
up with this technology are
just doing it by default.

00:18:35.161 --> 00:18:37.660
The older companies that have
built up data sets and methods

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:39.400
of processing data
over 30 or 40 years

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.530
aren't moving that fast on it.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:44.200
I think the algorithmic
improvements and the ability

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:45.890
to leverage the cloud and all
of the things that are happening

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:48.040
and just the huge new flood
of data that's arriving,

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.373
I think it's going to be a
question of flexibility to be

00:18:50.373 --> 00:18:53.336
how fast can your technical
workforce actually adapt

00:18:53.336 --> 00:18:54.960
and actually do things
in a better way.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.043
VINT CERF: Now we need to
arm wrestle a little bit

00:18:57.043 --> 00:19:00.330
because think about rapid
training on the stock market.

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.530
One of the problems with
a lot of those algorithms

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:05.920
is they all follow the same
thing like kids playing soccer.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.200
The ball is over here if
the market's going down,

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:08.850
they all sell.

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:10.512
If the market is
going up, the all buy.

00:19:10.512 --> 00:19:12.970
And the market goes like that,
you have a bang-bang control

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:16.510
algorithm so, you have
to be careful about fast

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:18.100
leading to instability.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:21.010
My question to you,
Marc, is how do you

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:25.678
