swings back and forth.

00:30:13.246 --> 00:30:14.750
We've seen it happen many times.

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:17.750
Now we're in the same place
where we were in the 1960s.

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:19.820
People would show pictures
of giant buildings

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:23.750
called the computing foundry
with smoke coming out

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:27.650
of the top and then everybody
went to the private machines

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:32.590
and departmental machines and
things like that and so it

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:34.340
keeps swinging back and forth.

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:36.740
When you start thinking about
the very low power things

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:40.700
that we use for mobiles and
other related IOT devices,

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:44.400
those may end up in the
computing environment

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:49.400
of the computing centers as
well simply because they are not

00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:50.000
power hungry.

00:30:52.844 --> 00:30:55.010
I don't think the clouds
are going to go away at all

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:56.510
I think what will
happen is the edge

00:30:56.510 --> 00:30:59.630
is going to keep getting more
and more and proliferated

00:30:59.630 --> 00:31:02.000
but the clouds are going to
have to be there in order

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:03.380
to make things more coherent

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:04.130
QUENTIN HARDY: OK.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:05.750
What kind of
structures do you think

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:08.387
could become fundamental
technologies, like DNS did?

00:31:08.387 --> 00:31:09.470
VINT CERF: I'm sorry say--

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:11.090
QUENTIN HARDY: What kind
of fundamental structures

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.610
do you think might become
important in the future?

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:15.500
VINT CERF: Probably the
most fundamental one

00:31:15.500 --> 00:31:20.150
has to do with the semantic
modeling of what's going on.

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:23.060
The thing which
makes AI powerful

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:28.220
is its ability to build
models that are analytic.

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:30.490
Right now we don't
do that very well.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:35.150
We have neural networks whose
function we don't understand.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:38.930
If there were a powerful
addition to the computing

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:40.490
environment that
we could point out,

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:43.280
it would be the
ability to extract

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:45.530
a model of the real
world. for example,

00:31:45.530 --> 00:31:48.290
or a model of an artificial
world like a company

00:31:48.290 --> 00:31:51.830
and then try to understand
how to reason about it.

00:31:51.830 --> 00:31:56.090
We don't have a vocabulary for
that yet, let alone grammar,

00:31:56.090 --> 00:31:58.092
so, if there is a
breakthrough somewhere,

00:31:58.092 --> 00:32:00.050
that's the one that I
think would find the most

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:00.440
interesting.

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:01.190
QUENTIN HARDY: OK.

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:02.690
Last question for
the both of you.

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:06.465
Biggest regrets and proudest
moments in your career?

00:32:06.465 --> 00:32:07.590
VINT CERF: I'm sorry, the--

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:10.131
QUENTIN HARDY: Biggest regrets
and proudest moments in your--

00:32:10.131 --> 00:32:11.750
VINT CERF: Wow.

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:14.870
I wish that I had used
IPv6 instead of IPv4

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:16.920
to begin with because--

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:19.020
QUENTIN HARDY: I meant
to bring that up.

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:21.974
VINT CERF: Who knew.

00:32:21.974 --> 00:32:23.640
And we probably could
have done a better

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:26.610
job of using public key crypto
except that the timing wasn't

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:27.210
very good.

00:32:27.210 --> 00:32:29.100
The paper that was
published in '76

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:30.960
didn't include an algorithm.

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:32.790
Then RSA comes along
a little bit later

00:32:32.790 --> 00:32:35.112
just as we freeze
the internet design

00:32:35.112 --> 00:32:37.320
and I want to get it
implemented so we can figure out

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:40.470
if it'll work, and so I put
off the public key stuff

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:41.070
for a while.

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:42.660
If it had been at
the right time,

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.300
we would have included
it except for one thing,

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:47.160
the internet was built
by graduate students

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:49.570
and you were all one of
those once upon a time.

00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:51.720
They're the most undisciplined
people in the world

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:54.180
and getting them to
use crypto properly

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:56.220
would have been
impossible anyway.

00:32:56.220 --> 00:33:00.360
It's too bad but that's an
alternate history I think.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:02.361
MARC ANDREESSEN: No regrets.

00:33:02.361 --> 00:33:03.860
QUENTIN HARDY:
You're shocked right?

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:05.860
MARC ANDREESSEN: I'm from
the Luke Hayes school,

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:07.680
always forward and
never backwards.

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:09.255
Proudest accomplishment
I got to say,

00:33:09.255 --> 00:33:10.838
the people I've been
able to work with

00:33:10.838 --> 00:33:12.491
and certainly Vint
is one of these

00:33:12.491 --> 00:33:14.490
but just the people I've
been able to work with.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:16.157
Especially now, younger
people coming up

00:33:16.157 --> 00:33:17.906
in the industry and
the ability for people

00:33:17.906 --> 00:33:20.610
to be able to realize their
dreams and create new things

00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:21.590
and be able to learn.

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.040
I think we're all in the
process of learning and growing

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:23.620
all the time.

00:33:23.620 --> 00:33:25.370
And I think our ability
to help each other

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:27.902
and teach each other in the
end, is the most important thing

00:33:27.902 --> 00:33:28.860
and the most rewarding.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:30.040
QUENTIN HARDY: That's a
really great note to end on.

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:32.370
I had some more questions
but we've run out of time.

00:33:32.370 --> 00:33:36.240
This was fascinating, high value
and we ended up over $3,000 so,

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:37.170
thank you so much.

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:38.295
MARC ANDREESSEN: Thank you.

00:33:38.295 --> 00:33:39.650
Thank you, Quentin.

